  <h1>How to calculate BMI by hand</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Body mass index is one division: your weight divided by your height squared. It needs
    only a scale, a tape measure and a pocket calculator, and doing it once by hand takes the
    mystery out of the number. This page shows both formulas, explains where the 703 in the
    imperial version comes from, and works three examples by hand.
  </p>
  <nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page"><p class="onpage-title">On this page</p><ul><li><a href="#the-short-answer">The short answer</a></li><li><a href="#the-formula-step-by-step">The formula, step by step</a></li><li><a href="#where-the-703-comes-from">Where the 703 comes from</a></li><li><a href="#two-examples-worked-by-hand">Two examples, worked by hand</a></li><li><a href="#checking-both-formulas-agree">Checking both formulas agree</a></li><li><a href="#the-healthy-weight-range-for-your-height">The healthy weight range for your height</a></li><li><a href="#five-mistakes-that-break-the-calculation">Five mistakes that break the calculation</a></li><li><a href="#what-the-number-cannot-tell-you">What the number cannot tell you</a></li><li><a href="#questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</a></li></ul></nav>

  <h2 id="the-short-answer">The short answer</h2>
  <p>
    In metric units, BMI is your weight in kilograms divided by your height in metres squared:
  </p>
  <p>
    BMI = weight (kg) ÷ height (m)²
  </p>
  <p>
    In imperial units, the same definition needs a conversion factor, because pounds and inches
    are not metric units:
  </p>
  <p>
    BMI = 703 × weight (lb) ÷ height (in)²
  </p>
  <p>
    Both versions give the same answer when the numbers are converted correctly. The result is
    rounded to one decimal place, then read against the World Health Organization bands for
    adults: under 18.5 is underweight, 18.5 to 24.9 is the healthy weight band, 25.0 to 29.9 is
    overweight, and 30.0 and over is obese. The number is a screening tool, not a diagnosis.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-formula-step-by-step">The formula, step by step</h2>
  <p>
    The calculation has three steps, whichever unit system you use:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write down your weight and your height in matching units.</li>
    <li>Square your height: multiply it by itself.</li>
    <li>Divide your weight by that number. In imperial units, multiply by 703 as well.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The one step people get wrong is the height conversion. The metric formula wants metres,
    not centimetres, so divide centimetres by 100 first: 170 cm is 1.70 m. If you would rather
    stay in centimetres, there is a matching version of the formula: BMI = weight (kg) ÷
    height (cm)² × 10,000. It gives the same answer, because 10,000 is 100 squared. The
    imperial formula wants total inches, not feet and inches, so convert first: 5 feet 9
    inches is 5 × 12 + 9 = 69 inches.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where-the-703-comes-from">Where the 703 comes from</h2>
  <p>
    The 703 is not a magic number and it is not a fudge factor. It is the number that converts
    pounds per square inch into kilograms per square metre, so the imperial formula lands on
    the same value as the metric one. One pound is 0.45359237 kilograms, and one inch is
    0.0254 metres, so one square inch is 0.0254² = 0.00064516 square metres. Divide the two:
    0.45359237 ÷ 0.00064516 = 703.07, rounded to 703. The rounding never moves a BMI reported
    to one decimal place by more than 0.01. If you ever want to check a conversion, multiply
    by 703.07 instead of 703: the third digit drops out of any rounded result.
  </p>

  <h2 id="two-examples-worked-by-hand">Two examples, worked by hand</h2>
  <p>
    Metric first. Someone who weighs 65 kg and is 170 cm tall:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">Working</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Convert height to metres</td><td>170 ÷ 100</td><td class="num">1.70 m</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Square the height</td><td>1.70 × 1.70</td><td class="num">2.89 m²</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Divide weight by that</td><td>65 ÷ 2.89</td><td class="num">22.49</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>Round to one decimal place</td><td>BMI</td><td class="num">22.5</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    22.5 sits inside the healthy weight band of 18.5 to 24.9. The centimetre version gives the
    same answer: 65 ÷ 170² × 10,000 = 65 ÷ 28,900 × 10,000 = 22.49, which rounds to 22.5.
  </p>
  <p>
    Imperial next. Someone who weighs 175 lb and is 5 feet 9 inches tall:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">Working</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Convert height to inches</td><td>5 × 12 + 9</td><td class="num">69 in</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Square the height</td><td>69 × 69</td><td class="num">4,761 in²</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Multiply pounds by 703</td><td>175 × 703</td><td class="num">123,025</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Divide</td><td>123,025 ÷ 4,761</td><td class="num">25.84</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>Round to one decimal place</td><td>BMI</td><td class="num">25.8</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    25.8 sits just inside the overweight band, which starts at 25.0. A second person at 200 lb
    and 5 feet 10 inches (70 inches) works out at 703 × 200 ÷ 4,900 = 28.7, further into the
    same band.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checking-both-formulas-agree">Checking both formulas agree</h2>
  <p>
    The two formulas are the same equation in different units, so the same person worked out
    both ways should land on the same number. Take someone who weighs 90 kg and is 183 cm
    tall. Metric: 1.83² = 3.3489, and 90 ÷ 3.3489 = 26.87, which rounds to 26.9. In imperial
    units the same person is about 198 lb and 72 inches tall: 703 × 198 ÷ 5,184 = 26.85,
    which also rounds to 26.9. If your two answers differ by more than 0.1, one of the
    conversions is off, and the height conversion is the usual suspect.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-healthy-weight-range-for-your-height">The healthy weight range for your height</h2>
  <p>
    The formula can be turned around. If BMI = weight ÷ height², then weight = BMI ×
    height². Put the two edges of the healthy band, 18.5 and 24.9, into that rearranged form
    and you get the weight range for any height. For someone 160 cm tall, height squared is
    1.60² = 2.56, so the range is 18.5 × 2.56 = 47.4 kg up to 24.9 × 2.56 = 63.7 kg. For
    someone 175 cm tall it is 56.7 kg to 76.3 kg. Treat the middle of the range as a rough
    landmark, not a target you owe anyone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="five-mistakes-that-break-the-calculation">Five mistakes that break the calculation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Forgetting to convert centimetres to metres.</strong> Dividing 65 by 170² gives
      0.0022, a number so small it is obviously wrong. A quick sanity check catches almost
      every error: a real BMI sits between about 10 and 60.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mixing the two systems.</strong> The 703 belongs only with pounds and inches. Use
      it with kilograms or centimetres and the answer is meaningless.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rounding before the end.</strong> Round your height to the nearest inch and your
      weight to the nearest pound before computing, and the errors can add up to about 0.3,
      enough to change your band at a boundary. Keep the full numbers until the last step,
      then round once.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Measuring height with shoes on.</strong> An extra couple of centimetres matters
      more than an extra kilo, because height is squared and weight is not. Measure without
      shoes, standing straight against a wall.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Using the adult bands for a child.</strong> Children and teenagers aged 2 to 19
      are judged against age and sex percentiles, not the adult cut-offs. The adult formula
      still gives a number, but the bands do not apply to it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-the-number-cannot-tell-you">What the number cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    BMI uses only height and weight. It does not measure body fat, and it cannot tell muscle
    from fat, so a rugby player and a sedentary office worker can share a BMI of 27 with very
    different health. It says nothing about where fat sits, and waist size matters: fat
    around the waist carries more risk than the same weight spread elsewhere. It also misses
    fitness, blood pressure and age. Doctors still use it because it is free, instant and
    roughly right across whole populations, but it is a first sort, not a verdict. The healthy
    range is drawn for adults of European background; several health bodies use lower action
    points, 23 and 27.5, for people of South Asian, Chinese and some other backgrounds,
    because risk rises earlier. If the result worries you, take it to a doctor, not to a diet
    you found online.
  </p>

