  <h2 id="questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>Do I round my height and weight before calculating?</dt>
    <dd>
      No. Keep the full numbers through the calculation and round once, at the end, to one
      decimal place. Rounding early can shift the answer by about 0.3, enough to change your
      band at a boundary.
    </dd>
    <dt>Why is my BMI different on different calculators?</dt>
    <dd>
      Usually rounding, or a height entered in the wrong units. Two calculators fed the same
      height and weight should agree to within 0.1. If they differ by more, check the height
      conversion first, then whether one of them rounded halfway through.
    </dd>
    <dt>Is the healthy range the same for everyone?</dt>
    <dd>
      No. The WHO bands are drawn for adults of European background. Several health bodies use
      lower action points, 23 and 27.5, for people of South Asian, Chinese and some other
      backgrounds, because risk rises earlier. Children use age and sex percentiles instead.
    </dd>
    <dt>Does BMI work for children?</dt>
    <dd>
      Not with the adult bands. Children and teenagers aged 2 to 19 are judged against
      percentiles for their age and sex, because their bodies are still growing and the same
      number means different things at different ages.
    </dd>
    <dt>My BMI is just over a boundary. Does that matter?</dt>
    <dd>
      Not much on its own. The bands are screening cut-offs, not lines where health changes
      suddenly. A BMI of 25.1 and a BMI of 24.9 describe almost the same person, and a small
      change in how you measured your height can move you across the line. What matters is
      the trend over time and the rest of your health, which is a conversation with a doctor.
    </dd>
    <dt>What is the centimetre version of the formula?</dt>
    <dd>
      BMI = weight (kg) ÷ height (cm)² × 10,000. It is the same formula with the metres
      conversion folded in: 10,000 is 100 squared. For 65 kg and 170 cm it gives 65 ÷ 28,900
      × 10,000 = 22.5, the same as the metres version.
    </dd>
  </dl>

